Date: Sat, 9 Jan 93 11:42:20 PST
From: ghsvax!hal@uunet.UU.NET (Hal Finney)
Subject: Re: Shamen, History, Wanking

David Jevans offers more points of discussion in his second posting.
Let me just point out that it is impossible to respond adequately to
postings that offer dozens of largely independent points, especially
when I disagree with the implicit assumptions and point of view behind
many of them.  (Despite David's claim, his posts are loaded with
opinions, in my opinion!)

I'll just pick one, since David explicitly asked for discussion:

> ...since we
> don't have a good understanding of conciousness, and we're not particularly
> intelligent, then how can we expect to be able to manage technologies that we
> develop?  My hypothesis is that our technical abilities outweigh our
> psychological abilities.  The development of our technologies have far
> outstripped our mental developments.  This causes me concern.  Can we have a
> discussion on this issue?

Let me make a few points.  First, this question is asked from a
collectivist perspective: "we" have to do this, "we" need to control
that.  But there is no such "we".  There are a lot of "I"'s, and they
will all have different opinions about what should be done and which
technologies should be used.  The problem is basically political, one
of balancing different interests and points of view.

The basic question to ask, from an extropian perspective, is, what
will free individuals, interacting without governmental restrictions,
do in terms of technological development?  Is the result guaranteed to
be disaster?  Can people not be permitted to live and work freely?  In
my opinion, freedom should be rejected only if it is very likely to
lead to very bad consequences.  That is why I objected to several
statements in David's earlier post about how freedom will inevitably
lead to terrible problems, in pollution and in nanotech.

Another point I'd make is that it's not a problem of lack of
understanding of consciousness or intelligence that leads to this
issue.  Even if we knew how to change consciousness, or increase
intelligence, we would still face the issue of destructive
technologies.  How would being smarter help the problem?  It might
make it worse - more people would be able to figure out how to build
nukes or nanotech eaters on their own.  And an angry smart person
could be more dangerous than an angry dumb person.

Maybe it seems like if we could "improve" our consciousness in some
ways this would not be a problem.  What if everyone could be
"improved" so that they cared more about others than themselves - a
world of pure altruists?  Perhaps in such a world no one would unleash
destructive technologies.

But such a world could have other "advantages" as well.  People would
no longer need to compete, they would happily work just to help each
other.  No one would starve because they would all be feeding each
other.  There would be no need to press forward with technological
development because there are no others who will out-compete you if
you don't.  Instead, a nicely static situation could be reached in
which the population was stable, technology is comfortable, and things
don't change.

Obviously I'm being satiric here.  Such a world would be a nightmare,
in my opinion.  Yet I'll bet that many people would find it
attractive.  I feel that it is our competitive nature which drives us
to improve ourselves, in keeping with extropian goals of self
transformation and boundless expansion.  It's hard for me to see why a
total altruist would have these desires.

Besides, how would such consciousness changes be implemented?  David
suggests that some evil group could create a nanotech germ which would
infect people and alter their brains.  Drexler mentioned the same idea
in Engines of Creation, and it is certainly something to worry about.
A solution we could hope for is to have nanotech immune systems to
fight off these nanotech invaders.

David also raises the suggestion that people could voluntarily refrain
from revealing new technologies they invent.  This might work
occasionally, but the problem is that scientific inventions have often
occured more or less independently to different groups.  If only one
person thinks of something, he might keep it secret.  But if he knows
that other people are on the track of the same idea, he has two
reasons not to keep it secret: first, it won't have helped if one of
the other groups goes ahead with the technology; and second, by
getting there first he may achieve various advantages.  So I don't
think it's reasonable to expect people to keep their discoveries
secret.

Nanotech and other new technologies will pose dangers, there is no
argument about that.  But trying to quash new discoveries is not
likely to work, and the power needed to even try to implement such
controls is likely to be misused in other ways, as we see today with
our governments.

Drexler writes at length on these issues in Engines.  One of his
solutions is one we haven't discussed here much, the Science Court.
This is designed to bring technological issues to people's attention,
so that they can start making informed decisions.  I think it's worth
discussing how a free society can deal with dangerous technologies.
Let's avoid David's assumptions about government restrictions until
we're sure that a free market can't work.

Hal Finney
hal@alumni.caltech.edu

