>From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)

>Terence Mckenna's mushrooms-as-message is interesting, but rather
>improbable.  Hallucinogenic molecules can't bear much information.  
>Embryological development of our genetic code can hold simple rules, 
>such as the "universal grammar" from which all human languages are 
>constructed, but it can't hold  anything so ambitious as the "secrets 
>of the universe".  Even if it did, or held something less ambitious 
>like a tuner for galactic alien Channel 3, mutation & selection would 
>have quickly wiped it out and replaced it with something more 
>conducive to the task of propagating genes in hunter-gatherer tribes.
   Two points. First, hallucinogenic mushrooms *do* seem to contain a
sort of "key" which opens the door to certain types of perceptions.
Perhaps not alien channel 3, but certainly some interesting, and
necessary for mental evolution IMHO, information about the imperfect
correspondance between our inner mental maps of the Universe and the
actual Universe. Of course, you can arrive at this knowledge by other
methods but hallucinigens are one way.
   Second, I'm not sure that evolution would wipe out a gene which was
totally dormant. If the gene was protected from random mutations
(from what I understand of new research, there are markers which can
act as check-sums built into some genes) then it might pass unnoticed.
Worse case, it could be linked to another gene so that any mutation
which altered the gene, caused an accompanying fatal mutation so that
only embryos with the marked gene survived.
   If the gene is dormant, it won't be selected for or against. Just
about every trait we humans have seems to have some survival value and
can be explained by selection. If a gene has no survival value at all,
neither good nor bad, *and* it is protected from corruption, there's
no reason why it couldn't stay in the gene pool.
   Betsy
PS for a cute story of another possible way to leave a message for the
ages, check out Carl Sagan's "Cosmos." This is a novel about a scientist
who works for years at a SETI installation, and the world's reaction
to the Message when it finally arrives. One of the few books I have
seen, BTW, with a female scientist as the main character, who is both
believable and admirable. 




