Date: Sat, 9 Jan 1993 15:13:04 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Neural transplants for the poor

A couple weeks ago I posted an article on crossloading (neural
transplant).  I'd love to hear comments on that.  Meanwhile,
the issue of poverty ("gap between rich and poor") has been
raised again, and I'd like to propose a solution for that
subset of the homeless, poor, etc. in their predicament
for chemical reasons (genetic, drugs, etc.).  

If neural grafting can treat Parkinson's, Alzheimer's, etc.
patients in the near future, then it might also be worthwhile
for those less neurally disadvantaged.  Grafting provides,
in the simpler case, replacement neurotransmitters, and in
the complex case, replacement or augmented neural function.

Statists might turn this into some sort of welfare program,
making it mandatory for welfare recipients to receive the
treatment, and funding it with taxes.  I suggest a voluntary
program could be funded with the "personal income shares" proposed 
a while ago.  Investors would provide the patient with funds for 
the grafting in exchange for a share of future income.  For best 
results, neural grafting might be provided as a package deal with 
vocational education.

Without income shares, education and neural grafting are 
much less effective for helping the poor.  And alas, in our 
unextropian society using medical procedures for enhancment is 
frowned upon, especially this kind of enhancment!

