Date: Sat, 9 Jan 1993 16:52:23 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Re: TECH/POL: A Technology Power Dive?

What is the point in saying "we need to proceed with haste" towards 
new technology?  Hal Finney calls this kind of we-talk "collectivist."
IMHO, that goes a bit far, as did an earlier thread where we learned 
that a joint-venture corporate raid is "collectivist". :-)  Still, in a 
capitalist society tech only advances quickly if people choose
to invest in it.  This comes from specific tech being profitable,
not from tech being globally desirable.  A group of technophiles 
complaining "technology should go faster", or a group of Luddites 
complaining "technology should go slower", may not be able to 
implement such global desires without invoking the force of government 
to _make_ people build faster or slower.

Done very cleverly, an altruistic group like Foresight Institute might 
have some impact.  From what I've observed, the nanotech meme plays a 
large role in demonstrating how cryonic rescitation could theoretically 
work, but I question whether it will accelerate the pace of technology.  
The "gray-goo" meme may even retard the pace of technology, just as the 
Heinlein's "Blowups Happen" came back forty years later as "The China 
Syndrome", foresighted SF fed Jeremy Rifkin's fears, etc.  I note that 
Drexler & Co. have been spending much energy trying to refute the "gray 
goo" scenario since he let that cat out of the bag!

Moving towards an independent extropian society would help. Much useful 
tech is being or will be delayed or banned by technophobic states:
cryptostuff, cryonics, human genetic therapy, neural transplants, 
psychoactives, etc.  It would be great to have a society, even a 
small Caribean island, where these kinds of services could be provided 
with quality.  See how "collectivist" we-think leads to "collectivist" 
solutions?  :-)

