> simulation that we can provide a VR with adequate volume.
 
> Hal Finney
 
My original post speculated that aliens had seized our solar system and
uploaded us without our knowledge  First, there's no need to upload every
being on the entire planet.  In fact, for all I know, I was the only one;
you are just a simulation.  Second, there's no need to run our VR at the
same rate as their's.  Maybe we're running a billion times slower, which
gives them plenty of time to fill in any needed detail, as well as correct
any contradictions which we might notice (including after the fact, by
altering our memories). 
 
Regarding your point as to whether VR can ever be as rich as physical
reality, there appears to me to be a tradeoff between detail of experience
and rapidity of thought.  If you want to experience a walk through the
forest followed by a gourmet meal, while I want to skip all the frills and
just stick to business, it's going to take you longer than me to have our
respective experiences.  (As an analogy, my ink-jet printer gives me a
choice between "fast" and "best.")
 
> There is a classic science fiction story, "Ker-Plop," about a
