Subject: SELF/LING:  Speed reading

While wading through dense philosophy texts, I often wish I could
zap through them at the speed my computer performs a spell-check, and
still understand the material.  Since we have yet to learn how to
endow people with such a capacity, I suppose we must settle for
the tedious, protoplasmic way for now.

I don't know if this has been discussed before, but does anyone have
any experience with any type of speed-reading techniques?  It would
be marvelous if I could read at twice the speed at which I currently
read (and I'm not especially fast), but I imagine the possibilities
of reading, digesting, and processing material at a few thousand w.p.m.
Ah, imagine reducing the torturous moments wading through
required academic readings. . . .  Zap through the _whole_ New York
Times in 3 or four minutes (though, really, would this be an instance
of self-improvement?).  Get through War and Peace in an hour or two. . .
And all this would leave time for more study and more
navel-contemplation. . . .   Of course, the key challenge is
to increase speed without decreasing enjoyment/understanding.  Anyone
know if this is a pipe dream with today's technology?  How do those
speed reading techniques work, and, do they _really_ work?

Brush and floss regularly,

