Date: Sun, 10 Jan 93 00:36:30 EST
From: dplatt@cellar.org (Douglas Platt)
Subject: Virtual Vision/Hip PC and TryChorder update

In response to Tom Morrow's recent post re the Virtual Vision system, here 
are my initial thoughts. After I've talked to someone at the company and 
received their literature, I'll post the accurate details, rather than the 
possibly dubious initial impressions that follow.

It does look as if it may be more fashionable than the Private Eye. OTOH it 
will probably not be as adjustable as the P.E. which I can position above or 
below my line of sight. Also, us glasses wearers will probably have a 
difficulties.

As to its possible uses as a computer output device, I doubt highly that the 
resolution is anywhere near as good as the P.E. which provides full 80x25 
text appearing as a 12" screen about 1.5 ft away. This device will probably 
be more comparable to a viewfinder in a camcorder, and while it might provide 
tv-like lifelike color images, I would be surprised if you could read the 
screen at more than 40x25.

Weight, power consumption and cost are not trivial disadvantages either. And 
it will not be trivial to connect it to a pc. Devices that do that for vga 
output cost a couple hundred bucks, are not tiny, and need yet another 
battery pack.

In other news........my trip to MIT went quite well, and I believe Marvin 
Minsky is now a chord keyboard convert. I stayed at his house, met some of 
his highly intelligent family, and bsd till almost 3am. Noticed a copy of 
Great Mambo Chicken, mentioned it, and found out the author Ed Regis had left 
it with him after doing an interview with him for his next book. He had not 
read it yet, so hopefully my recommendation will motivate him to check it 
out. The talk the next day was rather lightly attended (around 25) due to 
being during the semester break. But those there were seriouse heavyweights. 
Simpson Garfield, reporter and author, was there and will be doing an article 
about me in the Village Voice shortly. I will probably be speaking at MIT 
again in the Spring.

For those of you who have requested my qwerty to chordy software, TryChorder, 
and more info about the Hip PC, these will be coming shortly, hopefully 
by the end of January. Thanks for the interest, and patience. BTW TryChorder 
code has been written by Jay Skeer (extropian name Jay Prime Positive) who 
some of you know, and at one time was on this list.

Russell Whitaker called me on New Years Day from London to talk about this 
stuff. Thanks, Russell! I enjoyed developing scenarios in which by the end of 
93, several extropians are users of Wearable Information Technology Systems 
(W.I.T.S.) engaged in real time virtually telepathic conferences via wireless 
modems! Extropians....abandon your desks!

That's all for now.

EXTROPRENEURIALLY (who likes this futique neologism besides me?),

