Date: Sun, 10 Jan 1993 03:16-EST
From: Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU
Subject: What kinds of universes can you imagine?

The bit in Carl Sagan's "Contact" where they find a message encoded in
the billionth digits of Pi is pretty cool.  It raises some interesting
philosophical questions; but it got flamed a lot on sci.math by people
who feel that mathematics is somehow too fundamental for God to diddle
with.  Does it make any sense at all to talk about some sort of Creator
encoding a message in a fundamental arithmetic constant?  

It got me thinking, just how different a universe can I imagine?  Say
I'm thinking of making a simulator, along the lines of Conway's Game of
Life, with some extremely different rules.  How about a universe where
cause-and-effect doesn't apply?  Where the notion of counting is
useless and silly?  

I haven't come up with a good example.  It's so tempting to design a
universe with an initial state and a set of simple transition rules;
but that already builds in a lot of assumptions.  It's also necessary
to leave room for some interesting complexity to creep in.

A universe that consists entirely of one color?  A universe where time
doesn't exist, and instead the static universe is defined by the
solution to a minimization problem?  Where the rules change depending
on the phase of the moon?  

Help me out on this one.


-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)



